  "id": "COMPOUND:2248 - PROTEIN:3990",
  "verbalization": "Myricetin, a flavonoid compound, is implicated in the relationship with the Inner centromere protein through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Myricetin acts as a target for several compounds that, in turn, target the Inner centromere protein. Specifically, Myricetin is linked to Peripheral myelin protein 22, which is a target of 5-Hydroxymethyl-9-methoxycanthin-6-one, and both of these are targets of the Inner centromere protein. Similarly, Myricetin is also linked to Werner syndrome ATP-dependent helicase, which is a target of another compound that also targets the Inner centromere protein. Additionally, Myricetin is associated with Shiga-like toxin 1 subunit B, which is a target of (-) Carveol, both of which also target the Inner centromere protein. These findings collectively suggest that Myricetin may exert its biological effects through a series of molecular interactions that ultimately lead to the modulation of the Inner centromere protein. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Myricetin to influence cellular processes involving centromere function, though further experimental validation is required to confirm the direct role of Myricetin in the regulation of the Inner centromere protein.",
